 2. 🗳️ Fight night: Dems pile on Trump 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Mike Rogers, Republican nominee for U.S. Senate in Michigan (right), speaks during a televised debate with Democratic nominee Abdul El-Sayed at WOOD-TV in Grand Rapids, Mich., yesterday. Photo: Paul Sancya/AP President Trump wants midterms, 25 days from now, to be about him. Democrats couldn't agree more: In a trio of fiery Senate debates last night, the Democrats all glued their GOP opponents to Trump, his war and tariffs.

 Attacks in all three featured personal insults : 🚗 In Michigan, former Rep. Mike Rogers (R-Mich.) and Democrat Abdul El-Sayed repeatedly traded personal attacks, talked over each other and even continued arguing during a commercial break. ( AP ) 

 One of the most heated moments was during an exchange about Flock cameras : Rogers said they were useful for catching suspected terrorists, adding, "Why would that bother you? Well, if you have someone in your family that might be on the, on the watch list …" "El-Sayed, who is of Egyptian descent and would become the country's first Muslim senator if elected — and who has faced racist and Islamophobic attacks on the campaign trail — seemed to suggest in his response that Mr. Rogers was accusing him of being tied to terrorism," The New York Times notes . " Go ahead and say it," El-Sayed said. "You've been tiptoeing around it the whole time." U.S. Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.) and Rep. Mike Collins (R-Ga.) debate in Atlanta last night. Photo: Robin Rayne/ZUMA Press Wire 🍑 In Georgia, Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.) and Rep. Mike Collins (R-Ga.) faced off in "an hour-long brawl dressed up as a debate," The Atlanta Journal-Constitution reports . 

 Ossoff accused Collins of having a "childlike devotion" to President Trump and ties to white nationalists and neo-Nazis, saying, "You've hired half the Third Reich." Collins called Ossoff an out-of-touch "radical." U.S. Sen. Susan Collins (R-Maine) and her Democratic challenger, Troy Jackson, face off in Westbrook, Maine, last night. Screenshot: WMTW-TV 🦞 In Maine, Democrat Troy Jackson tried to tie Sen. Susan Collins (R-Maine) to Trump, while Collins tried to distance herself, as they sparred over the Iran war, energy costs and abortion rights.

 Debate takeaways . 
 
