 5. 💼 Immigration split-screen 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella (left) was among the tech pioneers who received awards from President Trump at yesterday's "A New Golden Age" summit at the U.S. Institute of Peace in Washington. Photo: Evan Vucci/Reuters The Trump administration said yesterday that it was suspending Microsoft from a key program used to sponsor employees for green cards. 

 Later in the day, the administration honored the tech giant's CEO with a medal for innovation. Why it matters: The split-screen highlights the tension between the administration's championing of Big Tech and its immigration policies, which constrain an industry that has long relied on hiring foreign-born workers, Axios' Emily Peck writes. 

 Tech leaders have said that restrictions on hiring hamper the country's ability to compete — especially at a moment when the U.S. is in a race with China on AI. Vice President Vance addressed the apparent contradiction at a press conference yesterday: "Our message to Microsoft is not that we want to harm your company; it's that we want your company to thrive, but we want it to thrive by employing and empowering your fellow Americans."

 Microsoft said 80% of the applications it submitted in the last fiscal year were to extend or change the status of existing employees — not to hire new people. Read on ... Brittany Gibson contributed reporting. 

 
 
